ok   unravel_index with shape () and index 0 -> ()
---- advanced indexing semantics
ok   a[arr1, :, arr2]: advanced indices separated by a slice -> broadcast dims FIRST: shape (2, 4), r[k, j] = a[i0[k], j, i2[k]]
ok   a[:, arr1, arr2]: adjacent advanced indices -> dims in place: shape (3, 2)
ok   a[(2,1) idx, :, (2,) idx]: broadcast (2,2) index dims first -> (2, 2, 4)
ok   an integer counts as an advanced index: a[arr, :, 1] -> dims first (2, 4) (docstring 'not x[arr1, :, 1]')
ok   a[1, :, arr] also puts the advanced dim first: shape (2, 4)
ok   a[..., arr] = take(arr, axis=-1)
ok   doc shapes: x[:, ind_1, ind_2] -> (10,2,3,4,40,50); x[:, ind_1, :, ind_2] -> (2,3,4,10,30,50)
ok   doc: x[..., ind, :] shape (10, 2, 5, 2, 30) for x (10,20,30), ind (2,5,2)
ok   boolean mask with fewer dims (3,4) on (3,4,5): result (count, 5), rows in C order of True positions
ok   1-D boolean on axis 0 = nonzero index; combined with a slice and an int array
ok   boolean mask of wrong length raises IndexError
ok   repeated indices in assignment: final value is one of the assigned values ('no guarantee for the iteration order')
   x[[1,1,3,1]] = [10,20,30,40] -> x[1] = 40.0 (last-wins observed: True; the docs promise no order)
ok   x[[1,1,3,1]] += 1 increments only once (docstring [0, 11, 20, 31, 40])
ok   np.add.at accumulates repeated indices ([0, 13, 20, 31, 40])
ok   np.add.at with a tuple of index arrays and values
ok   np.multiply.at accumulates; unary ufunc.at (negative twice = identity)
ok   negative indices count from the end; out-of-bounds raise IndexError (int and array)
ok   empty index array: a[[]] shape (0, 4, 5); a[:, []] shape (3, 0, 5); on an int array dtype preserved
ok   float index arrays raise IndexError
ok   advanced indexing returns a copy, basic slicing a view
ok   x[()] of a 0-d array returns a scalar; x[...] a 0-d view
---- nonzero / argwhere / where
ok   nonzero: row-major (C) order indices per dimension (docstring)
ok   1.17-2.0: nonzero on a 0-d array warns DeprecationWarning and behaves as atleast_1d
ok   argwhere: (N, ndim) grouped by element (docstring [[0,2],[1,0],[1,1],[1,2]])
ok   argwhere on 0-d: 'produces a result of the correct shape for a 0D array' -> (1, 0) / (0, 0)
ok   argwhere on an empty array -> (0, ndim)
ok   where 3-arg: result dtype = result_type(x, y) (int8 array, float32 array -> float32)
ok   where 3-arg: int64 array and float python scalar -> float64; values chosen elementwise
ok   where(uint8 array, python int 7) stays uint8 (both value-based 1.x and NEP 50 2.x)
